@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: the decision space around PROVEO_SBX=0 meaning "on the host" rather than "another container"
' would touch: internal/backend/** (a host backend does not exist yet — that absence IS the plan)
' would touch: internal/run/run.go (backend selection, buildPosture)
' would touch: internal/posture/posture.go (EnforcedBy, Observability)
' would touch: internal/credentials/**, internal/broker/** (whether brokering survives with no boundary)
' would touch: cmd/proveo/main.go (the flag or variable that would select it)
' session: b2e065e3-98e9-4974-ae9e-86990b8b1c81
' PLANNED. _spec/_plans/ is exempt from the SPEC-reference rule. Nothing here is
' cited from source because the mode described here DOES NOT EXIST. This file
' states a DECISION SPACE: measured facts are marked MEASURED, and everything
' else is a QUESTION. No design is chosen and no boundary is promised.
'
'   the plan that creates this .. _spec/_plans/retire-docker-egress.puml
'   what kept a container here .. _spec/_paradigms/retire-dind.puml (APPLIED)
'   what would be forfeited (1) . _spec/_paradigms/credential-boundary.puml
'   what would be forfeited (2) . _spec/_paradigms/egress-boundary.puml
'   what would be forfeited (3) . _spec/_paradigms/runtime-user-boundary.puml
'   what would be forfeited (4) . _spec/internal/sbx/clone-workspace.puml
'   why a host may not qualify .. _spec/internal/sbx/host-readiness.puml
'   the custody item downstream . _spec/_plans/secret-custody.puml (item d)
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE PLAN AND NOT A PARAGRAPH IN THE OTHER ───────────
' THE OPERATOR'S STATEMENT: `PROVEO_SBX=0` RUNS ON THE HOST.
'
' Today that is not what it does. MEASURED: `sandbox.Enabled()`
' (internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go:39) reads `PROVEO_SBX` and, on
' off/0/no/false/disable/disabled, hands the run to the docker+egress backend —
' `internal/backend/dockeregress`. That is a DIFFERENT CONTAINER, and every
' boundary proveo asserts still holds inside it.
'
' If docker+egress is retired (_spec/_plans/retire-docker-egress.puml), the
' escape hatch stops meaning "a different container" and starts meaning "no
' sandbox at all". That is not a smaller promise; it is a CATEGORICALLY
' DIFFERENT one, and it deserves its own file rather than a line in a
' retirement plan whose subject is something else.
'
' ── WHAT IS TRUE TODAY — MEASURED ──────────────────────────────────────────
' 1. THERE IS NO HOST BACKEND. `internal/backend` holds exactly two:
'    `dockeregress` and `sandbox`. Nothing in this tree runs an agent outside a
'    container or a VM. Everything below is therefore about a mode that would
'    have to be BUILT, not one that would be exposed.
'
' 2. THE VARIABLE ALREADY CARRIES OPERATOR EXPECTATIONS. `internal/run/
'    choices.go:239` tells the operator that "PROVEO_SBX=0 or --egress-mode
'    review fall back to docker + egress sidecars", and run.go:662 names it as
'    the way to reach Claude in Chrome. Those sentences describe a container.
'
' 3. A HOST CAN FAIL TO QUALIFY FOR A SANDBOX FOR REASONS NOBODY CHOSE.
'    host-readiness.puml (written today) hands the question "can this host run a
'    sandbox" to `sbx diagnose` rather than to proveo's own guesses, precisely
'    because the false-negative direction is expensive. A host with no KVM is a
'    concrete, common case; CI runners and nested-virt-less VMs are the same
'    shape. This is why the mode is worth ASKING about rather than dismissing.
'
' ── WHAT GOES AWAY, ALL AT ONCE — AND THE QUESTION THAT FOLLOWS ────────────
' Q1. EVERY BOUNDARY proveo ASSERTS IS GONE SIMULTANEOUSLY:
'       filesystem ...... no container, so no mount set; the agent sees the
'                         whole host filesystem the operator can see
'       egress .......... no MITM, no Squid, no Kit allowlist, no sandboxd
'       runtime user .... runtime-user-boundary's "never root, any host uid"
'                         becomes "whatever uid the operator is", including root
'       clone mode ...... clone-workspace exists so the sandbox edits a private
'                         clone; on the host the checkout IS the working tree
'     WHICH OF THESE, IF ANY, CAN BE PARTIALLY RECOVERED ON A HOST? Not "should
'     they be" — CAN they, honestly, with what a host offers? This plan does not
'     answer it and must not imply that the answer is yes.
'
' Q2. THE CREDENTIAL BOUNDARY INVERTS. credential-boundary.puml exists to keep
'     provider keys OUT of the agent — inject at the hop, strip off-route,
'     sentinel everywhere else. On the host the agent inherits the operator's
'     WHOLE environment and every file they can read, including the Keychain
'     items `security` will hand over and any `.env` in the tree. IS BROKERING
'     EVEN MEANINGFUL THERE — or does host mode simply FORFEIT it, and say so
'     loudly? A broker whose secret the agent can read from the environment two
'     seconds later is a ceremony, not a boundary.
'
' Q3. EGRESS ENFORCEMENT ON A HOST WOULD REST ON PROXY ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
'     THE AGENT CAN SIMPLY UNSET. `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY`/`NO_PROXY` are a
'     convention, not a boundary: the process they constrain is the process that
'     can rewrite them. IS THAT WORTH OFFERING AT ALL, or is an honest "no
'     enforcement" better than a boundary that LOOKS like one? egress-boundary's
'     three modes all assume a hop the agent cannot route around.
'
' Q4. WHAT DOES proveo STILL PROVIDE WITH NO SANDBOX? This is the case FOR
'     keeping the mode, so it has to be enumerated rather than assumed. The
'     candidates, each of which is a question about whether it still holds:
'       posture reporting ..... does a posture line that reports "no boundary"
'                               have value, or is it theatre?
'       model role resolution . the bridges and alias resolution are pure
'                               host-side logic; do they care about a container?
'       config seeding ........ seeding writes into a home the host already has
'       the run log ........... artifacts, transcript, exit code
'       git identity .......... authorship on the host is the OPERATOR'S, which
'                               is either the point or the problem
'     Enumerating this honestly is the only way to tell a legitimate mode from a
'     wrapper that pretends to be one.
'
' Q5. WHEN IS HOST MODE LEGITIMATE RATHER THAN A FOOTGUN? A host with no KVM is
'     now concrete (host-readiness.puml). CI runners and nested-virt-less VMs
'     are the same shape — and some of those are ALREADY disposable, which is a
'     real argument that the missing boundary was already provided by the
'     environment. Does proveo get to rely on that, and how would it know?
'
' Q6. HOW DOES THE OPERATOR KNOW WHICH MODE THEY ARE IN? This is the failure the
'     plan most needs to prevent: a run that SILENTLY DEGRADES from "sandboxed"
'     to "on your machine". retire-dind already rejected silence once —
'     `PROVEO_DIND` warns and no-ops rather than being read and ignored. The
'     open part is what the equivalent is here: a warning, a required flag, a
'     confirmation, or a refusal. Note that the defect recorded in
'     retire-docker-egress is the SAME shape — a posture line that describes an
'     intent nobody enforces — so this question is not hypothetical.
title PLANNED — PROVEO_SBX=0 with no container: what "fallback" would then mean

component "PROVEO_SBX=0 — today\nselects internal/backend/dockeregress" as TODAY <<app>>
component "PROVEO_SBX=0 — after retirement\nselects: nothing. The host." as AFTER <<error>>

node "the host, as the agent would see it" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "the agent process\nthe operator's uid, or root" as AGENT <<error>>
  component "the operator's environ\nevery key already exported" as ENV <<error>>
  component "the real checkout\nnot a clone; edits land at once" as TREE <<error>>
}

folder "boundaries that end simultaneously" as LOST <<host>> {
  component "filesystem\nno mount set to be outside of" as FS <<error>>
  component "egress\nno MITM, no Squid, no Kit allow" as EG <<error>>
  component "runtime user\nnever-root is no longer proveo's to say" as RU <<error>>
  component "clone mode\nthe checkout is the working tree" as CL <<error>>
}

folder "what may survive — Q4, to be enumerated" as KEEP <<host>> {
  component "posture reporting\nvalue only if it says NO boundary" as POST <<app>>
  component "model role resolution\nhost-side logic either way" as ROLES <<app>>
  component "config seeding + run log" as SEED <<app>>
  component "git identity\nthe operator's own, for once" as GIT <<app>>
}

component "proxy env vars\nthe agent can unset them" as PROXYVARS <<error>>
component "the broker\nkeys the agent can read anyway" as BROKER <<error>>
cloud "the provider / the open web" as NET <<cloud>>
component "a host with no KVM\nCI runners · nested-virt-less VMs" as NOKVM <<app>>
component "**silent degradation**\nsandboxed -> on your machine" as SILENT <<error>>

TODAY ARROW_ERROR AFTER : retire-docker-egress changes what this word means
AFTER ARROW_MAIN HOST
HOST ARROW_ERROR LOST
AGENT ARROW_MAIN ENV
AGENT ARROW_MAIN TREE
AGENT ARROW_CLOUD NET : direct — nothing in the path
BROKER ARROW_ERROR ENV : Q2 — brokering, or ceremony?
PROXYVARS ARROW_ERROR EG : Q3 — a boundary, or the look of one?
AFTER ARROW_OPTIONAL KEEP : Q4 — the case for keeping the mode
NOKVM ARROW_OPTIONAL AFTER : Q5 — the legitimate case
AFTER ARROW_ERROR SILENT : Q6 — the failure to prevent

note bottom of TODAY
  **MEASURED — today it is a CONTAINER.**
  ""sandbox.Enabled()"" reads ""PROVEO_SBX"" and
  falls back to ""internal/backend/dockeregress"".
  Every boundary proveo asserts still holds
  inside it. That is the promise being changed.
end note

note bottom of AFTER
  **MEASURED — no host backend exists.**
  ""internal/backend"" holds ""dockeregress"" and
  ""sandbox"", and nothing else. This mode would
  have to be BUILT, not exposed. That is why
  every line below is a question.
end note

note bottom of LOST
  **Q1 — they end together, not in order.**
  Filesystem, egress, runtime user and clone are
  four separate promises today and one absence
  tomorrow. Which, if any, can be PARTIALLY
  recovered on a host? Not answered here.
end note

note bottom of BROKER
  **Q2 — the credential boundary inverts.**
  Brokering keeps a key out of the agent. On the
  host the agent already has the operator's whole
  environment and every readable file. Is that
  worth doing, or is forfeiting it more honest?
end note

note bottom of PROXYVARS
  **Q3 — the constrained process holds the
  lever.** ""HTTP_PROXY"" is a convention the
  agent can unset. Is an unenforceable proxy
  worth offering, or is a stated "no
  enforcement" the safer thing to ship?
end note

note bottom of KEEP
  **Q4 — this is the case FOR the mode.**
  Posture, model roles, config seeding, the run
  log, git identity. Each has to be checked, not
  assumed: a value that only exists because of a
  container does not survive losing one.
end note

note top of POST
  **A posture line has a job here or none.**
  If it reports "no boundary" plainly it is the
  most useful thing left. If it prints a tier or
  an "enforced by", it repeats the exact defect
  recorded in retire-docker-egress.
end note

note bottom of NOKVM
  **Q5 — the legitimate case is concrete.**
  host-readiness gives ""sbx diagnose"" the
  question "can this host run a sandbox". A host
  that answers no is not a careless operator.
  Some are disposable already — does that count?
end note

note bottom of SILENT
  **Q6 — the failure this plan exists to
  prevent.** A run that degrades quietly from
  sandboxed to on-your-machine. Warning,
  required flag, confirmation, or refusal?
  retire-dind rejected silence once already.
end note

note bottom of TREE
  **Clone mode was protecting something.**
  On sbx the sandbox edits a private clone and
  the work is lifted back. On the host the first
  write is the operator's tree. Whether that is
  a loss or the point is Q1's hardest part.
end note

legend bottom
  **What this plan is, and is not**
  ==
  **IS** — the statement that ""PROVEO_SBX=0"" would stop meaning "another container",
  three measured facts about today, and six questions with no answers in them.
  --
  **IS NOT** — a design for a host backend, a claim that one should exist, or a promise
  that any boundary can be recovered outside a sandbox.
  ==
  **Its dependency**: this question only arises if _spec/_plans/retire-docker-egress.puml
  is acted on. That plan's Q6 asks for the migration signal; this file is what the signal
  would have to describe.
  --
  **Its inheritance**: retire-dind kept the docker backend as this escape hatch and said
  so. Deleting the backend without deciding what the hatch becomes is the one outcome
  both plans rule out.
end legend
@enduml
